.\" Carries no version string on purpose: the release bump touches eight files
.\" and this is not one of them. Keep it in step with HELP in the four CLIs.
.TH SHCL 1 2026-08-30 shcl "User Commands"
.SH NAME
shcl \- read, edit, validate and format Simple Hierarchical Config Language files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY shcl
.B get
.RI [ type ]
.RI [ options ]
.I FILE PATH
.YS
.SY shcl
.B set
.RB [ \-\-write ]
.RI [ options ]
.I FILE
.YS
.SY shcl
.B fmt
.RB [ \-\-write ]
.I FILE
.YS
.SY shcl
.B check
.RI [ options ]
.I FILE
.YS
.SY shcl
.B init
.BI \-\-schema= SCHEMA
.RB [ \-\-no\-banner ]
.YS
.SY shcl
.BR count | instances
.RI [ options ]
.I FILE PATH
.YS
.SY shcl
.BR help | version | about | donate
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shcl
is the reference command for SHCL, the Simple Hierarchical Config Language.
Everything the language bindings do, it does from a shell: typed reads, edits,
schema validation, formatting, and starter-config generation. It is one
dependency-free executable, so it doubles as a config probe for shell scripts,
makefiles and CI jobs.
.PP
Values are stored as plain text and coerced only when the reading side asks for
a type, so a file can never silently decide that
.B NO
means false. A malformed line is skipped or repaired with a diagnostic rather
than taking down the whole file, which is why a read of an unrelated path still
answers after a typo three lines up.
.PP
Stable diagnostic codes go to stdout and the English prose to stderr, so a
script can match on
.B E014
without parsing a sentence.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B get
Read one value, or an array of them, at
.IR PATH .
The type is chosen at the call site with a type option; the default is
.BR \-\-string .
A trailing
.RB [ * ]
in a path segment fans the read across every instance of that segment.
.TP
.B set
Apply edits to
.IR FILE ,
the base document, and print the canonical result \- or rewrite
.I FILE
in place with
.BR \-\-write ,
which creates
.I FILE
when nothing is there yet.
Simple values go in as repeatable
.B \-\-set
or
.B \-\-set\-literal
options; raw blocks, set-only-if-absent and removal go in as a write-ops script
on stdin. See
.B WRITE OPS
below.
.TP
.B fmt
Print the canonical form of
.IR FILE ,
or rewrite it in place with
.BR \-\-write .
Comments and their placement survive. With
.B \-\-layer
it prints the merged document.
.TP
.B check
Load
.I FILE
and print its diagnostics. With
.BI \-\-schema= SCHEMA
it also validates the file against a schema, which is itself an ordinary
.B .shcl
file. Exits 6 when anything was found at error severity.
.TP
.B init
Print a commented starter config generated from a schema: required fields live,
optional ones commented out, wildcard paths noted. The footer naming the format
and pointing at its spec is left out by
.BR \-\-no\-banner .
.TP
.B count
Print the number of instances at
.IR PATH .
.TP
.B instances
Print the instance values at
.IR PATH ,
one per line.
.TP
.BR help ", " version ", " about ", " donate
Print this command's help, its version, what SHCL is, or where to support it.
The first two are also
.BR \-h / \-\-help
and
.BR \-v / \-V / \-\-version ;
the last two are also
.B \-\-about
and
.BR \-\-donate .
The flag spellings are recognized anywhere in option position, after
.I FILE
included; only the value of a value-taking option and anything after
.B \-\-
are data. A bare
.B shcl
with no arguments prints the help and exits 0.
.SH TYPE OPTIONS
Valid on
.B get
only. The default is
.BR \-\-string .
.TP
.BR \-\-int ", " \-\-float ", " \-\-bool ", " \-\-datetime ", " \-\-string ", " \-\-raw ", " \-\-rawinfo
Read the value as that type.
.B \-\-rawinfo
reads a raw block's info-string \- the tag on its fence line \- rather than its
content.
.TP
.B \-\-array
Read the value as an array of the chosen type.
.SH OPTIONS
The subcommands each option belongs to are named in parentheses. An option a
subcommand does not use is a usage error, not something quietly ignored.
.TP
.BI \-\-default= VALUE
.RB ( get )
Value to print when the read comes back anything but Good. Implies
.BR \-\-on\-bad=default ;
for arrays it is substituted per bad slot.
.TP
.BR \-\-on\-bad= error | default | flag
.RB ( get )
What a failed read does.
.B error
fails loudly;
.B default
prints the default;
.B flag
prints the value anyway and reports through the exit code. The last is the
default.
.TP
.B \-\-slots
.RB ( get )
Prefix each output line with its slot status and a tab \- per element for an
array, per matched instance for a wildcard.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-banner
.RB ( init )
Leave out the footer naming the format and pointing at its spec.
.TP
.BR \-\-write ", " \-w
.RB ( fmt ", " set )
Rewrite
.I FILE
in place instead of printing to stdout. The write goes through a temp file and
a rename, so an interrupted run cannot truncate the config. On
.B set
a
.I FILE
that does not exist yet is created;
.B fmt
has nothing to format and reports it missing.
.TP
.B \-\-lossy
.RB ( fmt ", " set )
With
.BR \-\-write ,
rewrite even when the load dropped lines this write would delete. Without it
the write refuses and nothing is changed.
.TP
.BR \-\-strictness= loose | standard | strict
.RB "(all but " init )
How much the loader tolerates; also spelled
.BR 1 ,
.B 2
and
.BR 3 .
Default
.BR standard .
.B init
takes no strictness because a schema is a program artifact and always loads at
standard.
.TP
.BI \-\-schema= SCHEMA
.RB ( check ", " init )
The schema file. On
.B check
it adds
.BR V ###
diagnostics; on
.B init
it is what the starter config is generated from.
.TP
.BI \-\-layer= FILE
.RB ( get ", " fmt ", " count ", " instances ", " set )
Merge a lower-priority layer under
.IR FILE .
Repeatable; earlier means lower priority.
.B check
takes no layers, because diagnostics cite line numbers and a merged document
has no single file to number against \- pipe
.B shcl fmt
into
.B shcl check
instead.
.TP
.BI \-\-set= PATH = VALUE
.RB ( get ", " fmt ", " count ", " instances ", " set )
Override one path as the topmost layer, after every file. Repeatable. On
.B set
it is an edit to the document itself, so it persists with
.BR \-\-write .
.I VALUE
goes in as data: its type still follows the text, so
.B 8
is an int, but a comma or a quote in it is content rather than syntax.
.I PATH
ends at the first
.B =
outside quotes and brackets, so a selector may hold one.
.TP
.BI \-\-set\-literal= PATH = TEXT
.RB ( get ", " fmt ", " count ", " instances ", " set )
As
.BR \-\-set ,
except
.I TEXT
goes in as value syntax the way a file spells it \- so
.B ports=80, 443
writes a two-element array. An unquoted
.B #
ends the value, and text spanning lines is rejected.
.PP
Value options accept either spelling:
.BI \-\-default= VALUE
or
.BR "\-\-default " VALUE .
In the space form the next argument is taken as the value whatever it looks
like, so
.B \-\-default \-\-int
reads
.B \-\-int
as the default. Use
.B \-\-
to end the options when a
.I FILE
or
.I PATH
begins with a dash.
.PP
.I FILE
may be
.B \-
for stdin. With
.BR \-\-layer ,
.I FILE
is the highest file layer and each
.B \-\-layer
is merged under it in order;
.B \-\-set
applies last.
.PP
Some combinations are refused outright, each as a usage error.
.B \-\-write
with
.B \-\-layer
would fold the merged layers into the base file permanently.
.B \-\-write
with
.B \-\-set
outside
.B set
would persist what was given as a read-time override.
.B \-\-lossy
without
.B \-\-write
overrides nothing.
.B \-\-layer=\-
is not valid on
.BR set ,
where stdin already carries the ops script or the document.
.B \-\-raw
and
.B \-\-rawinfo
have no
.B \-\-array
form. And
.B \-
may be named only once across
.IR FILE ,
.B \-\-layer
and
.B \-\-schema
\- there is one stdin, so two names for it would each read part of a document.
.SH WRITE OPS
.B set
reads a write-ops script on stdin when no
.B \-\-set
or
.B \-\-set\-literal
option carries the edits. One op per line, fields separated by tabs:
.PP
.RS
.nf
int|float|bool|string|datetime<TAB>PATH<TAB>VALUE
<type>\-array<TAB>PATH<TAB>V1<TAB>V2...
<type>[\-array]\-default<TAB>...
literal[\-default]<TAB>PATH<TAB>TEXT
raw<TAB>PATH<TAB>INFO<TAB>CONTENT
empty<TAB>PATH
comment<TAB>PATH<TAB>TEXT
remove<TAB>PATH
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.B \-default
forms write only if the path is absent. String and raw values decode
.BR \en ,
.B \et
and
.BR \e\e ,
and a line starting with
.B #
is a script comment. A
.B raw
op's INFO may not hold a line break or an unquoted
.BR # ,
which would read back as a comment on the fence line.
.PP
.I FILE
given as
.B \-
follows stdin: it is the piped document when an option holds the edits, and an
empty base when the ops script has stdin instead.
.SH WRITING IN PLACE
Every subcommand that loads a document prints the load's diagnostics to stderr,
once per run, so a recovered-from typo is just as visible when the value reads
back fine and the result goes to stdout. An in-place write also refuses
outright, at exit 7, when the load dropped content the rewrite would delete
\- a typo does not cost the line above it.
.B \-\-lossy
is how to say it was meant.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Good.
.TP
.B 1
Usage or I/O error.
.TP
.B 2
The value is empty.
.TP
.B 3
The path was not found.
.TP
.B 4
The value is not readable as the requested type.
.TP
.B 5
Multiple instances matched where one was wanted.
.TP
.B 6
.B check
found errors, a strict load failed, or
.BR init 's
schema has faults.
.TP
.B 7
An in-place write was refused because the load dropped content the rewrite
would delete.
.B \-\-lossy
overrides.
.SH EXAMPLES
Read an int, with a fallback when the field is absent:
.PP
.RS
.nf
shcl get \-\-int server.shcl workers
shcl get \-\-int \-\-default=8 server.shcl thread\-pool
.fi
.RE
.PP
Count repeated fields, then fan one read across every instance:
.PP
.RS
.nf
shcl count server.shcl site
shcl get \-\-array \-\-slots server.shcl 'site[*].tls.hsts'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Validate against a schema and gate a build on the exit code:
.PP
.RS
.nf
shcl check \-\-schema=app\-schema.shcl app.shcl
.fi
.RE
.PP
Generate a starter config for your own users:
.PP
.RS
.nf
shcl init \-\-schema=app\-schema.shcl > app.shcl
.fi
.RE
.PP
Normalize and edit a file in place:
.PP
.RS
.nf
shcl fmt \-\-write server.shcl
shcl set \-\-write server.shcl \-\-set 'workers=8'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Validate a merged document, which
.B check
will not do directly:
.PP
.RS
.nf
shcl fmt \-\-layer=base.shcl site.shcl | shcl check \-\-schema=s.shcl \-
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
